WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.303
Welcome to the story deck
for Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:07.162
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:12.125
Instead of nine chapters one at a time,
we'll follow one case all the way through.

4
00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:16.492
A macro document, an invoice, is
opened on a ring 0 workstation,

5
00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.287
and tries to start encoded PowerShell.

6
00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:21.953
An attack surface
reduction rule blocks it,

7
00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:24.988
an incident opens, and a
custom detection finds it.

8
00:00:24.988 --> 00:00:28.770
It's fictional: every value comes
from the kit's test fixtures,

9
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.326
and every output on a slide is
what the kit really printed.

10
00:00:32.326 --> 00:00:36.956
The workbook is a draft awaiting
review, and the code is illustrative,

11
00:00:36.956 --> 00:00:40.160
tested against fixtures,
not a live tenant.

12
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:44.898
Here's the route.

13
00:00:44.898 --> 00:00:47.373
Scene one asks who may
respond to it at all.

14
00:00:47.373 --> 00:00:51.235
Scene two, whether the device
that opened it is even seen.

15
00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:53.985
Scene three, what
prevention does around it.

16
00:00:53.985 --> 00:00:58.785
Scene four follows the attack surface
reduction rule that blocked it, ring by ring.

17
00:00:58.785 --> 00:01:01.135
Scene five is the incident it raised.

18
00:01:01.135 --> 00:01:05.310
Scene six, the custom detection
that finds it, kept as code.

19
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.910
Scene seven, who else it touched,
and what Defender may do on its own.

20
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.972
Scene eight, the month's evidence.

21
00:01:11.972 --> 00:01:15.285
And scene nine takes the
detection live, test first.

22
00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:19.163
Each scene has a story slide, a
try-it slide with real output,

23
00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:22.440
and, for the first eight,
a one-question check.

24
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.665
Scene one starts before the macro runs.

25
00:01:28.665 --> 00:01:33.449
Whoever triages this incident will
read it through Microsoft Graph,

26
00:01:33.449 --> 00:01:35.128
on the endpoint GCC uses.

27
00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:39.727
And whoever contains it, by isolating
the device or running live response,

28
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:44.703
holds that power through a role in Defender's
unified role-based access control.

29
00:01:44.703 --> 00:01:47.076
The program's design has four roles,

30
00:01:47.076 --> 00:01:50.677
and only the Tier 0 role
holds advanced live response.

31
00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:54.915
Every role goes to a group that
Privileged Identity Management governs.

32
00:01:54.915 --> 00:01:58.914
So the kit declares every call with
its permission, refuses a session

33
00:01:58.914 --> 00:02:02.702
in the wrong tenant, and checks
the role export against the design.

34
00:02:02.702 --> 00:02:06.180
That supports least privilege
and account management.

35
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:10.655
Try it.

36
00:02:10.655 --> 00:02:14.343
From the kit's folder, run the
access check on the fixtures.

37
00:02:14.343 --> 00:02:18.305
The read passes: incidents come
back with no permission error.

38
00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:20.030
Then three failures.

39
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.981
The detection engineer role
holds advanced live response,

40
00:02:23.981 --> 00:02:28.980
which only Tier 0 may hold, so its role
row fails and the restricted row fails.

41
00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.028
And the responder role, the one that
would contain our macro's device, is also

42
00:02:33.028 --> 00:02:37.468
assigned to a standing group that Privileged
Identity Management doesn't govern.

43
00:02:37.468 --> 00:02:42.530
The legacy role is a warning: someone
records a decision or removes it.

44
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.092
Check one.

45
00:02:47.092 --> 00:02:49.633
An analyst's Graph session is still signed

46
00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:52.318
in to another tenant when
the live run starts.

47
00:02:52.318 --> 00:02:55.986
Does the kit read that tenant
and pass, refuse the run,

48
00:02:55.986 --> 00:02:59.568
sign in again on its own, or
fall back to the fixtures?

49
00:02:59.568 --> 00:03:01.990
The answer opens the next scene.

50
00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.690
The answer to check one:
the kit refuses the run.

51
00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.639
A session in the wrong tenant
would read someone else's estate,

52
00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:13.878
or nothing, and pass.

53
00:03:13.878 --> 00:03:18.428
Scene two asks whether the workstation
that opened the invoice is even seen.

54
00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:21.327
Defender can only block,
or raise an incident,

55
00:03:21.327 --> 00:03:24.540
for a device whose sensor
is onboarded and active.

56
00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.022
And coverage is counted against the
inventory, not against Defender's own list,

57
00:03:29.022 --> 00:03:32.677
because a device that never
onboarded would be invisible there.

58
00:03:32.677 --> 00:03:36.015
Our workstation is in ring
0, and it's protected.

59
00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:39.640
Elsewhere in the estate,
five devices are tickets.

60
00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.115
Try it.

61
00:03:44.115 --> 00:03:45.577
Run the coverage check.

62
00:03:45.577 --> 00:03:50.064
Ring 0, where our workstation
sits, is two of two protected.

63
00:03:50.064 --> 00:03:54.377
Across the estate, nine of fourteen
in-scope devices are protected,

64
00:03:54.377 --> 00:03:56.415
sixty-four point three percent.

65
00:03:56.415 --> 00:04:00.876
The tickets: a sensor with impaired
communications, an inactive sensor,

66
00:04:00.876 --> 00:04:05.109
a device that was never onboarded,
a server whose exception expired,

67
00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:08.965
and a device Defender reports
that the inventory doesn't list.

68
00:04:08.965 --> 00:04:14.170
Our macro was on a device the sensor sees,
so the next scenes can tell its story.

69
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:18.745
Check two.

70
00:04:18.745 --> 00:04:21.751
Defender reports a device,
onboarded and active,

71
00:04:21.751 --> 00:04:24.833
that the system component
inventory doesn't list.

72
00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:27.415
Is it protected and fine, out of scope,

73
00:04:27.415 --> 00:04:32.690
a ticket and an inventory finding,
or excepted until someone notices?

74
00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.365
The answer to check two: it's a
ticket, and an inventory finding.

75
00:04:40.365 --> 00:04:43.427
Scene three looks at the
prevention around our macro.

76
00:04:43.427 --> 00:04:47.471
A macro that starts PowerShell often
reaches out to the network next,

77
00:04:47.471 --> 00:04:50.877
and network protection is the
layer that blocks that call.

78
00:04:50.877 --> 00:04:52.981
Ring 0, where our workstation is,

79
00:04:52.981 --> 00:04:57.591
enforces network protection and
controlled folder access in block.

80
00:04:57.591 --> 00:04:59.365
Ring 2 is still auditing.

81
00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:03.790
But one ring 2 workstation reads
zero: network protection off.

82
00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.365
The same macro there
would meet one less layer.

83
00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:09.826
The kit checks every device's
state against its ring's stage,

84
00:05:09.826 --> 00:05:14.420
which supports malicious code
protection and configuration settings.

85
00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:18.895
Try it.

86
00:05:18.895 --> 00:05:20.420
Run the prevention check.

87
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.520
Three drifts.

88
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.870
Tamper protection off on
a ring 1 workstation.

89
00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.932
Network protection off on the ring 2
workstation, where its ring should be auditing.

90
00:05:29.932 --> 00:05:32.957
And datagram processing
on at the file server.

91
00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:37.067
Below, the week's events: network
protection audited and blocked,

92
00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:41.796
controlled folder access audited and
blocked, and one tampering attempt.

93
00:05:41.796 --> 00:05:44.912
A device with tamper
protection off and a tampering

94
00:05:44.912 --> 00:05:48.320
attempt in the same week is
an incident, not a ticket.

95
00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:52.982
Check three.

96
00:05:52.982 --> 00:05:56.371
Ring 1's plan says network
protection is in block.

97
00:05:56.371 --> 00:05:58.482
A ring 1 device reads two.

98
00:05:58.482 --> 00:06:00.883
Is that correct, because two means on?

99
00:06:00.883 --> 00:06:01.895
Is it drift?

100
00:06:01.895 --> 00:06:03.920
Not applicable on workstations?

101
00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.520
Or a server setting?

102
00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.545
The answer to check three: drift.

103
00:06:11.545 --> 00:06:14.070
Two is audit, and ring 1 should block.

104
00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.793
Scene four is the rule
that stopped our macro:

105
00:06:16.793 --> 00:06:20.620
block all Office applications
from creating child processes.

106
00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.890
In ring 0 it's in block, and
the fixture's events show

107
00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:27.320
it blocked twice on our
workstation, for the invoice.

108
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.532
In ring 1 it's in warn,
six of fourteen days.

109
00:06:30.532 --> 00:06:34.894
In ring 2 it's still auditing, with
one hit nobody has decided about.

110
00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.670
There, the same macro
would only be logged.

111
00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.341
The kit tracks each rule's stage per ring,

112
00:06:40.341 --> 00:06:44.145
says which can move, and writes
the exact value for Intune.

113
00:06:44.145 --> 00:06:46.720
That supports least functionality.

114
00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:51.195
Try it.

115
00:06:51.195 --> 00:06:52.945
Run the A S R check.

116
00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:56.120
Ring 0 is done: fourteen rules in block.

117
00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:01.870
Ring 1 has twelve ready for block; our
rule is held, six of fourteen warn days.

118
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.165
Ring 2 is ready to leave
audit, except our rule,

119
00:07:05.165 --> 00:07:08.995
with one audit hit that has
no exclusion or decision.

120
00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:11.582
Then print the value that moves ring 1.

121
00:07:11.582 --> 00:07:15.662
Every rule goes to one, block,
but our rule stays at six,

122
00:07:15.662 --> 00:07:18.033
warn, because its days aren't up.

123
00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:22.740
That's the exact string the change
board pastes into the ring 1 policy.

124
00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.402
Check four.

125
00:07:27.402 --> 00:07:31.603
Ring 3's plan puts the LSASS
credential-stealing rule in warn.

126
00:07:31.603 --> 00:07:34.345
Is it ready for block after fourteen days,

127
00:07:34.345 --> 00:07:38.160
a plan error, fine on
workstations, or not applicable?

128
00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.435
The answer to check four: a plan error.

129
00:07:44.435 --> 00:07:46.410
That rule doesn't support warn.

130
00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.335
Scene five is the incident.

131
00:07:48.335 --> 00:07:51.813
Defender correlates the
alerts into incident 1001:

132
00:07:51.813 --> 00:07:56.585
encoded PowerShell launched by an
Office application on one endpoint.

133
00:07:56.585 --> 00:08:01.349
High severity, so the guide's table gives
it a one-hour triage window, and the

134
00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:06.347
gated rung: a playbook prepares containment,
and a named approver releases it.

135
00:08:06.347 --> 00:08:08.736
It has an owner, so it's triaged.

136
00:08:08.736 --> 00:08:12.772
But another high incident has waited
three and a half hours with no one.

137
00:08:12.772 --> 00:08:17.920
Meanwhile the exposure check records
Security Exposure Management as

138
00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:23.180
not available in GCC: a disposition
with an interim path, not a failure.

139
00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:27.655
Try it.

140
00:08:27.655 --> 00:08:29.105
Run the incident check.

141
00:08:29.105 --> 00:08:35.255
Our incident, 1001, is high, three
hours old, with an owner: triaged.

142
00:08:35.255 --> 00:08:39.328
Incident 1002, also high,
three and a half hours,

143
00:08:39.328 --> 00:08:43.080
no owner, against a
one-hour window: overdue.

144
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:48.030
Incident 1003 is medium and still
inside its twenty-four hours.

145
00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:52.605
And 1005 was resolved with its
classification still unknown.

146
00:08:52.605 --> 00:08:56.248
The findings list is what the
security operations lead gets,

147
00:08:56.248 --> 00:08:58.142
each with its containment rung.

148
00:08:58.142 --> 00:09:02.510
The high one is gated: a named
approver releases containment.

149
00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.198
Check five.

150
00:09:07.198 --> 00:09:13.373
A Zero Trust Assessment test needs Security
Exposure Management, in a GCC tenant.

151
00:09:13.373 --> 00:09:17.385
Is it a fail, a pass because
Vulnerability Management covers it,

152
00:09:17.385 --> 00:09:22.230
skipped as not applicable with the
interim path, or not recorded at all?

153
00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.755
The answer to check five: skipped,
not applicable, with the interim path.

154
00:09:30.755 --> 00:09:33.317
A disposition, not a failed control.

155
00:09:33.317 --> 00:09:37.844
Scene six is the custom detection
that finds our macro's behavior:

156
00:09:37.844 --> 00:09:41.531
encoded PowerShell started
by Word, Excel or Outlook.

157
00:09:41.531 --> 00:09:43.462
It's Learn's own example rule,

158
00:09:43.462 --> 00:09:47.243
kept as a file in a repository,
with a test file beside it.

159
00:09:47.243 --> 00:09:50.853
Its known-bad window holds
a replay on our workstation;

160
00:09:50.853 --> 00:09:53.180
its known-good window is a quiet day.

161
00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:58.167
It's deployed disabled, and the file
now says enabled, after a passing test.

162
00:09:58.167 --> 00:10:01.229
So the plan updates its
status, and nothing else.

163
00:10:01.229 --> 00:10:04.834
A neighbor rule that would isolate
devices on its own is held:

164
00:10:04.834 --> 00:10:07.150
containment stays with a person.

165
00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:11.625
Try it.

166
00:10:11.625 --> 00:10:14.813
Validate, test and plan the repository.

167
00:10:14.813 --> 00:10:20.825
Our rule finds one row in its known-bad
window and none on its quiet day: it passes.

168
00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:23.137
The plan creates the dump-tool rule,

169
00:10:23.137 --> 00:10:26.325
and updates our rule's
status, and only its status.

170
00:10:26.325 --> 00:10:30.637
The admin-share rule is held, because
it would isolate devices on its own.

171
00:10:30.637 --> 00:10:33.525
And a legacy rule that
was made in the portal,

172
00:10:33.525 --> 00:10:36.413
and lives in no file, is
reported as an orphan.

173
00:10:36.413 --> 00:10:38.087
Then read the plan's hash.

174
00:10:38.087 --> 00:10:40.170
That's what an approver signs.

175
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:44.945
Check six.

176
00:10:44.945 --> 00:10:48.720
A rule file sets automated
actions to isolate devices.

177
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.891
Does the plan create it
because it's faster, hold it,

178
00:10:51.891 --> 00:10:55.260
create it disabled, or
delete the deployed copy?

179
00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.698
The answer to check
six: the plan holds it.

180
00:11:01.698 --> 00:11:03.547
Containment stays with a person.

181
00:11:03.547 --> 00:11:05.561
Scene seven asks two things.

182
00:11:05.561 --> 00:11:06.973
Who else did this touch?

183
00:11:06.973 --> 00:11:11.746
The guide's cross-source query finds users
with alerts from more than one sensor in

184
00:11:11.746 --> 00:11:14.273
a week, and an emergency
access account goes

185
00:11:14.273 --> 00:11:16.660
to the top for the incident response lead.

186
00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.848
And what may Defender do on its own?

187
00:11:18.848 --> 00:11:21.677
Ring 0's device group
is at full remediation,

188
00:11:21.677 --> 00:11:25.372
so Defender may clean up our
workstation automatically.

189
00:11:25.372 --> 00:11:26.973
A server group never is.

190
00:11:26.973 --> 00:11:31.634
And every emergency access account must
be excluded from attack disruption,

191
00:11:31.634 --> 00:11:34.900
so an incident can't lock
out the people who fix it.

192
00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:39.375
Try it.

193
00:11:39.375 --> 00:11:40.975
Run the automation check.

194
00:11:40.975 --> 00:11:44.460
Ring 0's workstations are at
full, as the ladder says,

195
00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.613
so our workstation's
cleanup can run on its own.

196
00:11:47.613 --> 00:11:52.812
Ring 3's workstation group is at a semi
level with no exception: off the ladder.

197
00:11:52.812 --> 00:11:56.101
The scanner subnet's exclusion
passed its review date.

198
00:11:56.101 --> 00:11:59.289
The second emergency access
account isn't excluded,

199
00:11:59.289 --> 00:12:01.662
so attack disruption could contain it.

200
00:12:01.662 --> 00:12:05.440
And one device runs a sensor
too old for Contain User.

201
00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:10.128
Check seven.

202
00:12:10.128 --> 00:12:13.615
The servers device group is
set to full remediation.

203
00:12:13.615 --> 00:12:18.493
Does it pass because that's Microsoft's
recommendation, fail because the

204
00:12:18.493 --> 00:12:23.480
ladder allows servers only a semi
level, pass in ring 0, or go unchecked?

205
00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:29.755
The answer to check seven: it fails.

206
00:12:29.755 --> 00:12:32.780
The ladder allows servers
only a semi level.

207
00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.330
Scene eight is the month.

208
00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:38.785
One command runs every check,
records each run with its controls,

209
00:12:38.785 --> 00:12:43.155
indicators and its own result,
and computes the guide's metrics.

210
00:12:43.155 --> 00:12:46.013
Our detection counts
in detection validity:

211
00:12:46.013 --> 00:12:48.717
one of one enabled rule
with a passing test.

212
00:12:48.717 --> 00:12:53.124
Ring 0's two workstations,
including ours, are the only devices

213
00:12:53.124 --> 00:12:57.530
with every rule in block, so
enforcement depth is two of eleven.

214
00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.920
And if anyone edits a recorded file
afterwards, the check names it.

215
00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:06.395
Try it.

216
00:13:06.395 --> 00:13:07.383
Run the month.

217
00:13:07.383 --> 00:13:11.572
Ten records, and only signals and
the metrics pass their own checks;

218
00:13:11.572 --> 00:13:15.457
every other run found something,
as the earlier scenes showed.

219
00:13:15.457 --> 00:13:20.097
Read three metrics: protected coverage
sixty-four point three percent,

220
00:13:20.097 --> 00:13:24.421
enforcement depth two of eleven,
and detection validity one of one.

221
00:13:24.421 --> 00:13:28.358
Then append one line to a recorded
tickets file, and check again.

222
00:13:28.358 --> 00:13:30.695
Both checks fail, and name the file.

223
00:13:30.695 --> 00:13:33.880
That's what makes this
evidence and not a folder.

224
00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.355
Check eight, the last one.

225
00:13:39.355 --> 00:13:42.421
The coverage run is
recorded with intact files,

226
00:13:42.421 --> 00:13:44.780
but its own check found five tickets.

227
00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:48.549
Does it pass the go or no-go
because the files are intact,

228
00:13:48.549 --> 00:13:52.420
fail, pass with a warning,
or pass only in production?

229
00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.083
The answer to check eight: no.

230
00:13:58.083 --> 00:14:02.482
A step passes only when its files
are intact and its own check passed.

231
00:14:02.482 --> 00:14:05.657
Scene nine takes our
detection live, test first.

232
00:14:05.657 --> 00:14:10.632
The same commands run in test and
production; only the settings file changes.

233
00:14:10.632 --> 00:14:13.375
The pre-flight checks the session's cloud,

234
00:14:13.375 --> 00:14:16.682
tenant and scopes, a first
read, and the output root.

235
00:14:16.682 --> 00:14:19.182
Publishing runs as a dry run first.

236
00:14:19.182 --> 00:14:23.945
The approver signs the plan's hash, and
only then is it applied with that hash.

237
00:14:23.945 --> 00:14:27.901
The smoke test replays the macro's
behavior on a ring 0 device,

238
00:14:27.901 --> 00:14:30.670
and the custom detection raises its alert.

239
00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.720
The rollback is planned before go.

240
00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:35.830
None of this has been run
against a live tenant.

241
00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:43.080
Try it, in fixture mode,
as the runbook prints it.

242
00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.467
Publish without apply.

243
00:14:44.467 --> 00:14:49.280
The plan would create the dump-tool rule,
and patch our rule, its status only.

244
00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:50.455
Nothing is sent.

245
00:14:50.455 --> 00:14:54.605
Then read the plan's hash, which the
approver records in the change record.

246
00:14:54.605 --> 00:14:58.691
Only with apply and that exact hash
does anything go to the tenant,

247
00:14:58.691 --> 00:15:01.793
and a plan that changed
after approval is refused.

248
00:15:01.793 --> 00:15:05.022
And the go or no-go after
the first run: pre-flight,

249
00:15:05.022 --> 00:15:10.655
publish and the scheduled run pass, but
five steps fail until their findings close.

250
00:15:10.655 --> 00:15:12.470
No-go, as it should be.

251
00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:18.233
That's our macro, end to end.

252
00:15:18.233 --> 00:15:20.794
Who may respond, through least privilege.

253
00:15:20.794 --> 00:15:24.395
A device the sensor sees,
counted against the inventory.

254
00:15:24.395 --> 00:15:26.183
Prevention checked by ring.

255
00:15:26.183 --> 00:15:28.919
The rule that blocked it,
followed ring by ring.

256
00:15:28.919 --> 00:15:31.320
An incident owned inside its window.

257
00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:35.445
A detection kept as code, shown
to fire and to stay quiet.

258
00:15:35.445 --> 00:15:37.433
Automation inside the ladder.

259
00:15:37.433 --> 00:15:39.345
The month's evidence in a chain.

260
00:15:39.345 --> 00:15:42.108
And the detection taken live, test first.

261
00:15:42.108 --> 00:15:43.245
Now it's your turn.

262
00:15:43.245 --> 00:15:46.499
Each chapter has a second
lab in your own test tenant,

263
00:15:46.499 --> 00:15:48.845
never production, with a back-out step.

264
00:15:48.845 --> 00:15:52.289
Then go deeper: the chapters
and their narrated tours,

265
00:15:52.289 --> 00:15:55.420
the learning path, and
the implementation guide.

266
00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:56.590
Thanks for watching.
